   "sp:advertisementStatus": "advertised, not included",
   "sp:targetClassAtSnapshot": "emitted-rhizome",
   "sp:note": "A DOCUMENTARY-AUTHORITY SETTLEMENT AT SCALE. The Domitianic consensus is a claim about which witness counts, decided by whom, and at what point it stopped being questioned — the same four powers this body measures, applied to a chronology rather than a form. Its primary external anchor is a late and ambiguous reception-history witness, and that is an admissibility question. This body does not adjudicate it."
  },
  {
